<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles III of Spain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1716">1716</a>–<a href="/years/1788">1788</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Charles of Bourbon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, previously king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, ruled Spain from 1759 to 1788. An enlightened reformer, he promoted roads, trade, academies and royal factories and expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1767. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon Reforms</a> created the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, the Captaincy General of Venezuela and the Internal Provinces, and introduced intendants.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain.jpg" alt="Charles III of Spain" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton Raphael Mengs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_III_of_Spain_high_resolution.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was born on 20 January 1716, the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V of Spain</a> and the eldest son of Philip's second wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabeth-farnese">Elisabeth Farnese</a>. His mother, who hoped he would have a kingdom of his own, made him heir to the Italian duchies of Parma and Piacenza, and in 1731, at fifteen, he became their duke. At eighteen he led Spanish troops down <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in an almost bloodless march to take the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, entering Naples on 10 May 1734. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vienna-1738">Treaty of Vienna</a> of 1738 confirmed him in both kingdoms. In Naples his chief minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-tanucci">Bernardo Tanucci</a>, pursued a regalist policy that limited the privileges of the clergy, whose lands were exempt from tax.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Charles became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on the death of his childless half-brother Ferdinand VI on 10 August 1759, and gave up the thrones of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. He ruled as an enlightened absolutist and relied on capable ministers, among them the Marquess of Esquilache, the Count of Campomanes, the Count of Aranda and the Count of Floridablanca. He widened trade, modernised agriculture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a>, promoted science and university research, and strengthened the army and navy.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the Family Compact of 15 August 1761, and the British captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> in 1762–63, though both were returned in the peace of 1763. To pay for a standing army and fortified ports, the sales tax, the alcabala, was raised from 2% to 5%. Bread riots in 1766, the Esquilache Riots, drove out the minister, and in 1767 Charles expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> from Spain and its empire, confiscating their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> and closing their colleges.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon Reforms</a> sought to bring the overseas territories under firmer control. The Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, set up in 1776, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a> and what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>, so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> flowed towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. In 1780 an indigenous rising led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a> broke out against colonial rule in the Viceroyalty of Peru.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles died on 14 December 1788, months before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> began. The historian Stanley Payne judged him probably the most successful European ruler of his generation. His reforms mostly raised revenue and extended state power, though he never gained complete control of the finances. His reign has been called the apogee of empire, which was not sustained after him: his son Charles IV continued some of his policies, but was forced to abdicate by his own son Ferdinand VII and was imprisoned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>, who invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1808.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles III of Spain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the last Farnese duke, Antonio, died in 1731, the duchy passed to <b>Charles of Bourbon</b>, son of Elisabeth Farnese, queen of Spain, under the European settlement of the Quadruple Alliance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles of Bourbon</b> won the kingdom at the Battle of Bitonto in 1734, but the army of his son Ferdinand IV collapsed against the French in 1798.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1734–1735 <b>Charles of Bourbon</b> conquered Naples and Sicily and was crowned in Palermo, uniting the two crowns in one person but keeping separate kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…school system was later copied abroad; Joseph II freed peasants from personal serfdom and granted toleration to Protestants and Jews; <b>Charles III of Spain</b> (1759–1788) promoted science, trade and agriculture; and in Portugal the Marquis of Pombal directed the rebuilding of the capital after the 1755 Lisbon earthquake.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Rouvray, north of Orléans, French and Scottish forces under <b>Charles of Bourbon</b> and Sir John Stewart of Darnley attacked a supply convoy bound for the English army besieging the city.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-12">12 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles III</b> (1759-1788) promoted roads, trade reform and enlightened ministers and expelled the Jesuits.</blockquote><a href="/state/bourbon-spain">Bourbon Spain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1762" aria-label="Madrid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles III of Spain"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4759 4110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4759 4110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1762">What was here in 1762? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
